The assessment is written to session_plan/assessment.md (about 9 KB). The commit step did nothing: session_plan/ is in .gitignore, so the git add … || true command dropped it, and I didn't force it in. The file is on disk for the planner.

Top findings for the planner:
• #1026 is decided but not built. On 2026-11-03 @yuanhao wrote the rule:
  • If the user set max_tokens themselves and the reply hits that limit, exit 0 with the stderr note.
  • If it hits a default limit they never chose, exit non-zero.
  • A refusal also exits non-zero.
  • json and stream-json output get the note too.

  Nothing implements it yet. final_stop_hit_output_limit is only used inside its own file, and no commit since Nov 3 mentions #1026. Claude Code fixed the same kind of bug on Nov 4 (claude -p reporting success when a reply was cut off). It has the most value for the least deciding.
• #1127 is still open. Run with no arguments, blame, docs, ast and memories still print REPL slash syntax in the shell (for example Usage: /blame). I only tried the bare form, not their error branches.
• Two files are close to failing the size gate. One more change to either can turn cargo test fatal and revert the whole task:
  • src/commands_risk_unhittable.rs is 2036 lines and not on the grandfathered list; 14 more lines is fatal.
  • src/config.rs is 4851 lines against its recorded 4762; 11 more lines is fatal.
• The #1114 repro no longer reproduces. yoyo -p --print-system-prompt now goes into prompt mode instead of printing the system prompt. What's left on the ticket is the value lookups like flag_value.

Other checks:
• Binary: -p returned PONG with exit 0.
• Malformed config: a broken .yoyo.toml makes -p refuse with exit 2, naming lines 1 and 2. This bug class is already handled.
• Run history: the last 4 evolve runs passed, the last 10 sessions completed both tasks each, and there were no reverts in 14 days.
• Research: I saved how Claude Code 2.1.318's fixes map onto yoyo to yopedia (job fa4f9dee). One contrast to note: when the CA certificate file is bad, Claude Code uses whatever certificates it can read and warns, while yoyo's check refuses to start.
